      faqs: [{"q":"What is an image rotator and flipper?","a":"An image rotator turns a photo by 90, 180, or 270 degrees, while a flipper mirrors it horizontally or vertically. Together they fix sideways, upside-down, and mirrored photos. A browser tool applies the change locally with HTML5 Canvas, so you can preview the result and save a corrected copy without installing software."},{"q":"How do I rotate image online without uploading my photo?","a":"Use a browser tool that processes the image locally, such as the free Image Rotate and Flip tool. Open the page, choose your image, click rotate left or right, preview the result, and download it. Because the work happens in your browser, the file is never sent to a server."},{"q":"What is the difference between rotating and flipping an image?","a":"Rotation turns the whole image around its centre, so the top becomes a side or the bottom. Flipping mirrors the image, swapping left and right or top and bottom. A sideways photo needs rotation, while a mirrored selfie with backwards text needs a horizontal flip."},{"q":"Can I turn a photo black and white with this tool?","a":"Yes. The grayscale filter converts a colour photo to black and white, keeping shades of grey from black to white. It works well for printing, portraits, and designs that need a neutral image. Colour information is removed, so use it when the look matters more than the original colours."},{"q":"Does rotating an image reduce its quality?","a":"Rotation by 90, 180, or 270 degrees rearranges pixels cleanly, so it does not blur the image. Quality can fall slightly if the result is saved as a JPEG and then saved again many times, because JPEG compression is repeated. For one rotation and save, the loss is not noticeable in normal use."},{"q":"Why does my photo look sideways in one app and upright in another?","a":"That usually comes from EXIF orientation data, which tells apps how the camera was held. Some apps read that data and some ignore it, so the same file displays differently. Rotating the photo and saving a new copy writes the pixels in the corrected orientation, so it should display consistently afterwards."}],
